      "heading": "K-means: alternate between assigning and moving",
      "narration": "The workhorse clustering algorithm is k-means, and it is two lines of idea. First you decide how many groups you want — that is k, and you must supply it. Scatter k points at random into your data space and call them centroids. Now repeat two steps until nothing changes. Step one, assign: give every data point to whichever centroid is nearest to it. Step two, move: shift each centroid to the mean position of all the points that were just assigned to it. Then reassign, because moving the centroids may have changed who is nearest to what. Then move again. Keep going until the assignments stop changing, and you are done. That is the whole algorithm, and you could implement it in twenty lines. There is a small piece of theory worth knowing, because it explains both why it works and what it cannot promise. Each of those two steps can only decrease the total distance from points to their assigned centroids — assignment decreases it by definition, and moving to the mean is exactly the point that minimizes squared distance. Since the quantity only falls and cannot fall forever, the algorithm always converges. But it converges to a local minimum, and which one you land in depends entirely on where the random centroids started. Two different seeds can give you two genuinely different clusterings of the same data. So the standard practice is to run it ten times with different initializations and keep the result with the lowest total distance. Now the genuinely hard part, which no algorithm solves for you: choosing k. The standard approach is the elbow method. Run k-means for k equals one, two, three, and so on, and plot the total within-cluster distance against k. It drops steeply at first — each new cluster explains a lot — and then flattens out, because you are now just splitting groups that were already coherent. The bend in that curve is a defensible choice of k. Silhouette score, which measures how much closer each point is to its own cluster than to the next nearest, is a useful second opinion. Neither is a verdict; k is ultimately a decision about what granularity is useful to you. Finally, initialization is important enough that it has its own algorithm. K-means-plus-plus chooses the starting centroids to be spread far apart rather than uniformly at random, which dramatically reduces bad local minima. It is the default in every serious library, and you should leave it that way.",
