      "heading": "What k-means assumes — and when to reach for something else",
      "narration": "K-means is fast, simple and everywhere, and it has strong opinions about what a cluster looks like. Knowing those opinions is what stops you from misusing it. First, and by now predictably: it works on distances, so you must scale your features. If one column is measured in millions and another in fractions, the clusters will be defined entirely by the first column, and everything else is decoration. Same trap as k-NN, same one-line fix, and it catches people just as often. Second, and this is the deep one: k-means minimizes squared distance to a mean, which means it implicitly assumes clusters are roughly round, roughly the same size, and roughly the same density. When those assumptions hold, it is excellent. When they do not, it fails in ways that look confident. Give it two long curved bands lying side by side, and it will slice straight across them, because a straight-ish boundary is what minimizing distance to two centres produces. Give it one tight little cluster sitting beside one enormous diffuse one, and the big one will swallow the small one's edge. The algorithm is not confused; it is faithfully optimizing the thing you asked it to optimize, and that thing happens to encode roundness. Third, every point must belong to some cluster. There is no option for 'this one is just weird'. So a single genuine outlier does not get ignored, it gets absorbed, and it pulls its centroid away from where the real group sits. If you expect noise in your data — and in real data you should — that alone is a reason to consider something else. So what else? The usual alternative is DBSCAN, and it thinks about clusters in a completely different way: a cluster is a region of high density, and you grow it by walking from point to nearby point wherever the neighbours are dense enough. That gives you three things k-means cannot. It finds clusters of any shape, including those two curved bands. It discovers the number of clusters itself rather than making you supply it. And it explicitly labels sparse points as noise rather than forcing them into a group. Its own weakness is that it assumes a single density threshold, so it struggles when some clusters are much denser than others. That is the real lesson of this slide, and it generalizes far beyond clustering: every algorithm encodes assumptions about the shape of the answer, and your job is to match those assumptions to your data rather than to run the default and believe it.",
