      "heading": "PCA: rewrite the data along its own axes",
      "narration": "Now the second half of the lesson: reducing dimensions. Suppose Nami measures each shell a hundred different ways — length, width, thickness, weight, twenty curvature measurements, colour channels, and so on. She now has a hundred numbers per shell, and she cannot look at a hundred-dimensional plot. But those hundred numbers are not a hundred independent facts. Long shells tend to be heavy. Wide shells tend to be thick. Most of those measurements are moving together, which means the true structure in her data lives in far fewer directions than a hundred. PCA finds those directions, in order of importance. The first principal component is the single direction in which the data varies the most — the long axis of the cloud. The second is the direction of maximum remaining variance among directions perpendicular to the first. The third is perpendicular to both, and so on. Then you re-express every data point in these new coordinates, and because the components are ordered by how much variance they capture, you can simply keep the first few and throw away the rest. And here is where lesson four pays for itself. Those directions are not found by search or optimization — they are exactly the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix of your data. Equivalently, and this is how it is actually computed, they are the right singular vectors from the SVD of your centred data matrix. And each eigenvalue is precisely the amount of variance captured along its eigenvector. So the whole method reduces to a decomposition you already know, and the eigenvalues hand you the answer to 'how many components do I keep?' — plot the cumulative proportion of variance explained and keep enough components to reach ninety or ninety-five percent. Two practical requirements. Centre your data, always, by subtracting the mean of each feature; PCA is about directions of variance around the centre, and if you skip this you get the direction of the mean instead. And scale your features unless they genuinely share units, because PCA chases variance and an unscaled column measured in millions will trivially become the first component regardless of whether it means anything. Both of these are one line each and both are skipped constantly.",
